Suppose:(case1:Integer)

List<Integer> nums = new ArrayList<>();

nums.add(30);
nums.add(10);
nums.add(20);

Now:

Collections.sort(nums);

Java can sort this because Integer already has a natural ordering.

So:

30
10
20

     ↓ Collections.sort(nums)

10
20
30

You don't need to create a Comparator yourself.

Why?

Because Integer already implements Comparable<Integer> internally.

Conceptually:

Integer
   ↓
implements Comparable<Integer>
   ↓
compareTo()
   ↓
natural integer ordering


2. Now give your own Comparator

Suppose:

Collections.sort(nums, com);

Now you're saying:

"Don't use the default/natural ordering. I am giving you a separate comparison rule called com. Use that rule."

For example, suppose com sorts in descending order:

Comparator<Integer> com = (a, b) -> Integer.compare(b, a);

Then:

Collections.sort(nums, com);

produces:

30
20
10

So your understanding here is correct:

Collections.sort(list, comparator) allows me to provide my own comparison logic.
----------------------------------------

Case 1: Student has no comparison logic → Comparator provides it

You have:

class Student {
    int age;
    String name;
}

There is no compareTo() inside Student.

Now you create a separate Comparator:

Comparator<Student> com = (s1, s2) ->
        Integer.compare(s1.age, s2.age);

Then:

Collections.sort(students, com);

The flow is:

Student class
    ↓
No comparison logic
    ↓
Comparator<Student>
    ↓
compare(s1, s2)
    ↓
Compare students by age

So yes:

The Comparator contains the comparison logic, and we pass that Comparator to Collections.sort().

Therefore Student does not need to implement Comparable.

Case 2: Student itself provides the comparison logic → Comparable

Now you say:

"I want Student to have its own natural/default ordering."

Then:

class Student implements Comparable<Student> {

    int age;
    String name;

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Student that) {
        return Integer.compare(this.age, that.age);
    }
}

Now:

Collections.sort(students);

That's enough.

You don't provide a Comparator.

Why?

Because Collections.sort(students) knows:

"These Students implement Comparable, so I'll use their compareTo() method."

The flow is:

Student
   ↓
implements Comparable<Student>
   ↓
compareTo(Student that)
   ↓
Student provides its own natural ordering
   ↓
Collections.sort(students)

